theory.intro:
When an object is fully submerged, the water level rises. The increase in the container’s **volume reading** shows how much water the object displaces. Compare the readings in mL, using the same container and without spilling water.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large},draw=black]
\definecolor{waterBlue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\fill[waterBlue!15,opacity=0.45] (0,0) rectangle (2,1.1);
\draw (0,3.2)--(0,0)--(2,0)--(2,3.2);
\draw[waterBlue] (0,1.1)--(2,1.1);
\node[above,font=\small] at (1,1.1) {start level};
\node[below,font=\small] at (1,0) {Before};
\fill[waterBlue!15,opacity=0.45] (4,0) rectangle (6,1.9);
\draw (4,3.2)--(4,0)--(6,0)--(6,3.2);
\filldraw[fill=white,draw=black] (5,0.55) circle (0.3);
\draw[waterBlue] (4,1.9)--(6,1.9);
\node[above,font=\small] at (5,1.9) {new level};
\node[below,font=\small] at (5,0) {After};
\draw[dashed] (2,1.1)--(6.6,1.1);
\draw[<->] (6.6,1.1)--(6.6,1.9);
\node[right,font=\small] at (6.7,1.5) {water level rises};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
